~~~javascript
function shortestPath(graph, start, target) {
  if (start === target) return [start];
  const queue = [start], parent = new Map([[start, null]]);
  let head = 0;
  while (head < queue.length) {
    const node = queue[head++];
    for (const next of graph.get(node) ?? []) {
      if (parent.has(next)) continue;
      parent.set(next, node);
      if (next === target) {
        const path = [];
        for (let at = target; at !== null; at = parent.get(at)) path.push(at);
        return path.reverse();
      }
      queue.push(next);
    }
  }
  return null;
}
const g = new Map([['A',['B','C']],['B',['D']],['C',['D']],['D',[]]]);
console.log(shortestPath(g,'A','D')); // ['A','B','D']
~~~
The graph maps each vertex to its neighbors; use both directions for undirected edges. BFS finds a minimum-edge path in an unweighted graph. It returns null when unreachable, and runs in O(V+E) time and space.

~~~javascript
class Node {
  constructor(value) { this.value=value; this.left=null; this.right=null; }
}
class BinarySearchTree {
  constructor() { this.root=null; }
  insert(value) {
    if (!this.root) { this.root=new Node(value); return; }
    let n=this.root;
    while (true) {
      if (value===n.value) return; // ignore duplicates
      const side=value<n.value?'left':'right';
      if (!n[side]) { n[side]=new Node(value); return; }
      n=n[side];
    }
  }
  search(value) {
    let n=this.root;
    while (n) {
      if (n.value===value) return true;
      n=value<n.value?n.left:n.right;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
const tree=new BinarySearchTree();
[8,3,10,1,6].forEach(v=>tree.insert(v));
console.log(tree.search(6));  // true
console.log(tree.search(12)); // false
~~~
Smaller values are placed left and larger values right. Operations take O(h), where h is the tree height; this plain BST is not balanced, so worst-case operations are O(n).

~~~cpp
#include <vector>

std::vector<int> mergeSorted(const std::vector<int>& a,
                             const std::vector<int>& b) {
    std::vector<int> out;
    out.reserve(a.size() + b.size());
    std::size_t i = 0, j = 0;
    while (i < a.size() && j < b.size()) {
        if (a[i] <= b[j]) out.push_back(a[i++]);
        else out.push_back(b[j++]);
    }
    while (i < a.size()) out.push_back(a[i++]);
    while (j < b.size()) out.push_back(b[j++]);
    return out;
}
~~~
For example, merging {1,4,7} and {2,4,6} yields {1,2,4,4,6,7}. Inputs must already be sorted in ascending order. The algorithm takes O(n+m) time and O(n+m) space for its result.

C++ exceptions report exceptional failures. Put potentially failing work in try, signal an error with throw, and handle a matching type in catch. During stack unwinding, local objects are destroyed.
