Pass-by-value gives a function a copy, so changing the parameter leaves the caller’s variable alone. Pass-by-reference gives the function an alias to that variable, so modifications affect the caller.
~~~cpp
#include <iostream>

void incrementCopy(int value) { ++value; }
void incrementOriginal(int& value) { ++value; }

int main() {
    int n=5;
    incrementCopy(n);
    std::cout << n << '\n'; // 5
    incrementOriginal(n);
    std::cout << n << '\n'; // 6
}
~~~
A const reference, such as const std::string&, avoids copying a large object while preventing modification. Use a reference when the function should act on the caller’s object; use value when an independent copy is desired.

~~~java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class BalancedBrackets {
    public static boolean isBalanced(String text) {
        Deque<Character> stack=new ArrayDeque<>();
        Map<Character,Character> opening=Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');
        for (char c:text.toCharArray()) {
            if (c=='('||c=='['||c=='{') stack.push(c);
            else if (opening.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop()!=opening.get(c)) return false;
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isBalanced("([]{})")); // true
        System.out.println(isBalanced("([)]"));   // false
    }
}
~~~
Save as BalancedBrackets.java, compile and run with javac BalancedBrackets.java and java BalancedBrackets. Non-bracket characters are ignored. The stack makes runtime and space O(n). Map.of requires Java 9 or later.

```javascript
function factorial(n) {
  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 0) {
    throw new RangeError("n must be a non-negative integer");
  }
  if (n === 0 || n === 1) return 1;
  return n * factorial(n - 1);
}

console.log(factorial(5)); // 120
```

The base cases `0! = 1` and `1! = 1` stop the recursion. Each other call reduces `n` by one, and the results multiply on the way back out. This direct version is easy to read, but a large input creates one stack frame per recursive call. JavaScript engines impose a finite call-stack limit, so sufficiently large `n` throws a `RangeError` (“Maximum call stack size exceeded”). Also, factorial values grow quickly beyond the range of precise JavaScript `Number` integers; use `BigInt` if you need exact larger results.

Here is a C++17 function that flattens a nested vector represented with a recursive variant. A nested value is either an integer or a list of nested values; the recursion handles any depth.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <variant>
#include <vector>

struct Node;
using List = std::vector<Node>;
struct Node : std::variant<int, List> {
    using variant::variant;
};

void flattenInto(const Node& node, std::vector<int>& output) {
    if (const auto* value = std::get_if<int>(&node)) {
        output.push_back(*value);
        return;
    }
    for (const Node& child : std::get<List>(node)) {
        flattenInto(child, output);
    }
}

std::vector<int> flatten(const List& input) {
    std::vector<int> result;
    for (const Node& node : input) flattenInto(node, result);
    return result;
}

int main() {
    List nested{1, List{2, List{3, 4}, 5}, 6};
    for (int n : flatten(nested)) std::cout << n << ' ';
}
```

Compile with `g++ -std=c++17 flatten.cpp -o flatten`. The example prints `1 2 3 4 5 6`. Runtime is O(N) for N total nodes, with recursion depth proportional to the nesting depth.

```java
public class Prefix {
    public static String longestCommonPrefix(String[] words) {
        if (words == null || words.length == 0) return "";
        String prefix = words[0];
        for (int i = 1; i < words.length && !prefix.isEmpty(); i++) {
            while (!words[i].startsWith(prefix)) {
                prefix = prefix.substring(0, prefix.length() - 1);
                if (prefix.isEmpty()) return "";
            }
        }
        return prefix;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(longestCommonPrefix(
            new String[] {"flower", "flow", "flight"})); // fl
    }
}
```

The function starts with the first string as a candidate and shortens it until each subsequent string begins with that candidate. If there are no strings, or no shared prefix, it returns the empty string. Compile with `javac Prefix.java`, then run `java Prefix`.

```java
public class CapitalizeWords {
    public static String capitalizeWords(String sentence) {
        if (sentence == null || sentence.isEmpty()) return sentence;
        StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder(sentence.length());
        boolean beginningOfWord = true;

        for (char ch : sentence.toCharArray()) {
            if (Character.isWhitespace(ch)) {
                result.append(ch);
                beginningOfWord = true;
            } else {
                result.append(beginningOfWord ? Character.toUpperCase(ch) : ch);
                beginningOfWord = false;
            }
        }
        return result.toString();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(capitalizeWords("hello, new world!"));
        // Hello, New World!
    }
}
```

This preserves the original spacing and punctuation and uppercases the first non-whitespace character after each whitespace run. It does not lowercase the rest of each word.

```python
def brackets_balanced(text: str) -> bool:
    """Return True if (), [], and {} are properly balanced and nested."""
    opening = "([{"
    matching = {")": "(", "]": "[", "}": "{"}
    }
    stack = []
    for char in text:
        if char in opening:
            stack.append(char)
        elif char in matching:
            if not stack or stack.pop() != matching[char]:
                return False
    return not stack

print(brackets_balanced("([]{})"))  # True
print(brackets_balanced("([)]"))    # False
```

The stack remembers unmatched opening brackets. A closing bracket must match the most recently opened one, which enforces correct nesting. Any non-bracket characters are ignored; if you want to reject them, add an `else` branch returning `False`.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

void permute(std::string& text, std::size_t index) {
    if (index == text.size()) {
        std::cout << text << '\n';
        return;
    }
    for (std::size_t i = index; i < text.size(); ++i) {
        std::swap(text[index], text[i]);
        permute(text, index + 1);
        std::swap(text[index], text[i]); // restore for the next choice
    }
}

int main() {
    std::string text = "cat";
    permute(text, 0);
}
```

Compile with `g++ -std=c++17 permutations.cpp -o permutations`. The program prints all permutations. If the string has repeated characters, this swap-based version can print duplicate permutations; for distinct outputs, sort the string first and use `std::next_permutation` in a loop. There are `n!` permutations, so the output grows rapidly.

