Java is **always pass-by-value**. The subtlety is that when you pass an object, the value being copied is a reference to that object. The method can use that copied reference to modify the same object, but reassigning the parameter does not change the caller’s variable.

```java
class Box {
    int value;
    Box(int value) { this.value = value; }
}

public class PassingDemo {
    static void changePrimitive(int x) {
        x = 99; // changes only the local copy
    }

    static void changeObject(Box b) {
        b.value = 99; // mutates the shared Box
    }

    static void reassignObject(Box b) {
        b = new Box(7); // changes only the local copy of the reference
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int number = 3;
        changePrimitive(number);
        System.out.println(number); // 3

        Box box = new Box(3);
        changeObject(box);
        System.out.println(box.value); // 99
        reassignObject(box);
        System.out.println(box.value); // still 99
    }
}
```

In languages with true pass-by-reference, a function can receive an alias for the caller’s variable itself and replace what that variable refers to. Java does not provide that behavior for method parameters. The phrase “objects are passed by reference” is misleading: Java passes the reference **by value**.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

bool isValidEmail(const std::string& email) {
    static const std::regex pattern(
        R"(^[A-Za-z0-9.!#$%&'*+/=?^_`{|}~-]+@[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{0,61}[A-Za-z0-9])?(?:\.[A-Za-z0-9](?:[A-Za-z0-9-]{0,61}[A-Za-z0-9])?)+$)"
    );
    return email.size() <= 254 && std::regex_match(email, pattern);
}

int main() {
    std::cout << std::boolalpha
              << isValidEmail("name@example.com") << '\n'  // true
